# DESCRIPTION

The core is what a native GPU driver implements and what its clients are written on: Loinnir (loinnir(2)) and the Vulkan layer.
It has one model, whatever a GPU can do:

- one bindless heap;
- one root pointer;
- command lists as data;
- image layouts that are the device's own;
- one timeline.

What a GPU can do, the core says, and a backend that lacks something says so in `DeviceFeatures` or `DeviceLimits`.
It names no graphics API.
It is Swift, tier 0.
A backend is a class that conforms to `GPUDevice` and is its own `GPUQueue`; there are two:

- `VulkanDevice` (graphics/core/vulkan) runs the core over Vulkan: Venus's under QEMU, or a host's own.
- `MaliDevice` (graphics/core/mali) runs it on a Mali GPU's command streams, through the `csf` capset of the GPU class (mali(3)), with MaliCore's recipes and the compiler's Valhall backend (valhallbackend(2)).

A program that wants Loinnir's whole library takes `Loinnir`, which picks the backend by what the GPU class offers: a GPU that offers `csf` gets `MaliDevice`; any other, `VulkanDevice` over Venus.

Every addition to the core has a default that means what the core did without it: a new field of a descriptor, a new argument of a call, a new requirement of `GPUDevice`.
A requirement a backend doesn't implement refuses with `.unsupported`.
What a client wrote before an addition compiles and runs as it did.

## What is refused, and how

An error is of one of two kinds, and they stay apart.
`.invalid` is a shape that is wrong on every device: a region outside its image, formats a copy can't go between, a draw outside a pass, a depth test with no depth attachment.
`.unsupported` is a shape that is right, which this device can't do: a feature its `DeviceFeatures` lack, a limit it is past, or a format it doesn't offer for that use.
Shape is judged first, so a list or a descriptor that one device runs and another refuses is the other's refusal of a feature, never of its shape.
The words of the error say what.

The rules are written once, in the core, and every backend calls them: `GPUCommandList.validate` for lists, and `check(limits:)` of `ImageDescriptor`, `SamplerDescriptor`, `GraphicsPipelineDescriptor` and `ImageView` (`check(for:limits:)`) where a backend makes the thing.
`validate(features:)` judges a list by the features alone, and `validate(limits:)` also by the limits and the formats' capabilities (a blit's formats, a pass's sample counts).

## Memory

`allocate` gives memory by what it is for: `.upload` is written by the CPU and read by the GPU, `.device` is the GPU's alone, `.readback` is written by the GPU and read by the CPU.
A `GPUMemory` has a 64-bit GPU `address`, which shaders follow, and for `.upload` and `.readback` a CPU `pointer` to the same bytes.
An image with the `scanout` usage (a render target) is one a display or a window shows as it is: `exportHandle(_ image:)` gives a new handle to its VMO, for a display to pin and read (the rights to transfer, read and measure it), where its rows are `width * 4` bytes; `exportWindowHandle(_ image:)` one a window may map, read and import, likewise; the Mali backend maps it as the GPU and its reader share it, and does no cache maintenance over it.
On the Mali backend `.readback` memory, and an image that is drawn into and copied out of and never copied into, are mapped cached by the CPU, so reading them is as fast as memory; the backend cleans and invalidates their caches in `wait(_:)`, so what the CPU reads after it is what the GPU wrote.
`free` and every `destroy` are safe at any time: the device keeps the resource until the work submitted before the call has finished.

`importHandle` is the other half of `exportHandle`: it makes memory, or an image, over the pages of a handle (a VMO) another process gave, with the device mapping its own reference.
It is `DeviceFeatures.importHandle`; where the device can't, it is `.unsupported`.
A window's frames, drawn by one process and composed by another, move this way: one VMO by ownership, never a copy of its pixels.

`makePresentImage(format:width:height:usage:)` makes the image a window shows (`PresentImage`): the one a program draws into, with `usage`, and the VMO the window is given (`exportHandle(_:)` of the `PresentImage`, a new handle each call), whose rows are `stridePixels` apart, which the window is told (sdk(2)'s `addImage` `stride`); `bytes` is `stridePixels * height * 4`.
The format is a four-byte colour format; Radharc reads blue first, so `bgra8` and `bgra8srgb` are the ones that show right.
There are two ways to the VMO, and the device says which it takes.
Where it presents direct (`DeviceFeatures.presentDirect`, the Mali backend), the image is `scanout` and `isDirect`, its own pages are the window's (`exportWindowHandle(_:)`: a VMO the window maps, reads and may import, and its row stride as the device laid it out), and presenting it adds nothing; a frame the device can't share so is `.unsupported`, never staged.
Otherwise `recordPresent(into:)` adds the GPU's copy of the image (made a copy source) into host-visible memory (`staging`, `width * height * 4` bytes, rows of `width` pixels as a copy into memory writes them) that the device exports, and the window is given that: the way under Venus, where an image's pages are the host's, in its tiling.
The call throws `.unsupported` if the device exports neither.
The window shows a frame once the timeline reaches the value of the work on it: where the device has `timelineHandle()`, that counter is each window image's acquire counter and a present names the value, so nothing waits on the CPU; elsewhere the program waits for the value before it presents.
`destroy(_ frame:)` gives the image and its staging memory back.

## Formats

A `PixelFormat` names a texel's layout.
The core has:

- colour: `r8`, `rg8` and `rgba8` in unorm, snorm, uint and sint (the unorm ones are `r8`, `rg8` and `rgba8`, the others `r8snorm`, `rg8uint` and so on), `rgba8srgb`, `bgra8` and `bgra8srgb`; `r16`, `rg16` and `rgba16` in float, uint and sint; `r32`, `rg32` and `rgba32` in float, uint and sint; `rgb10a2unorm`, `rg11b10float` and `rgb9e5`;
- depth and stencil: `depth16`, `depth32f`, `depth24stencil8`, `depth32fstencil8` and `stencil8`;
- compressed: `astc` and `astcSRGB` of each of the fourteen `ASTCBlock` sizes, ETC2 and EAC (`etc2rgb8`, `etc2rgb8a1`, `etc2rgba8` and their sRGB forms, `eacR11` and `eacRG11`, each unorm or snorm), and the BC formats (`bc1rgb` to `bc7srgb`).

A format describes itself.
`texelBlock` is the block's width and height in texels and its size in bytes (one texel, for an uncompressed format), as the Vulkan specification gives them; a combined depth and stencil texel is the whole texel's size (`depth32fstencil8` is five bytes), and `bytesPerTexel(aspect:)` is one aspect's, as a copy that names an aspect counts.
`aspects` says what a texel holds (`color`, `depth`, `stencil`), `isCompressed` and `family` which compressed family it is in, `numericType` how a channel is read, and `byteSize(width:height:depth:aspect:)` how many bytes a tightly packed extent takes, whole blocks to a row.
`bytesPerPixel` is a texel's bytes for an uncompressed colour format, and 0 for a compressed one.

`DeviceLimits.formats(_:)` is what a device does with a format: `FormatCapabilities` says whether it can be sampled, filtered, a render target, blended into, a storage image, a storage image with atomics, a depth target, or the source or destination of a blit, and the sample counts it has for each use (always including one).
A format the device doesn't list has none of these, and no image of it is made.
A backend fills the table at start (`DeviceLimits.setFormat`).
Which of the compressed families (ASTC, ETC2, BC) a device has is `DeviceFeatures`, apart from each format's capabilities.

## Images and views

An image has a format, a size (`width`, `height` and, for a 3D image, `depth`), `mipLevels`, `layers`, `samples` and a `dimension` (`d2`, `d3`, `cube` or `d1`; a cube is a 2D array whose layers are six to a cube, and the image is square; a 1D image is one row of texels, with layers where it is an array), and a usage: `sampled`, `renderTarget` (a colour attachment), `depthStencilTarget`, `storage`, `copySource`, `copyDestination` and `scanout`.
An image of one mip level, one layer and one sample, as a 2D image, is the image the core had; the other fields default to it, and the device refuses (`.unsupported`) what it lacks: mips, layers, cubes, volumes, multisampling, a depth or compressed format.
A multisampled image is 2D, has one mip level and an uncompressed format.
A 1D image is `DeviceFeatures.images1D`; its height and depth are 1, it has one sample and a colour format that isn't compressed, and it is sampled, stored to and copied but never drawn into, so a device that has them takes `sampled`, `storage`, `copySource` and `copyDestination` of it and refuses (`.invalid`) a render target, a depth target or a scanout.
A device picks the layout and tiling and tracks the layout itself: nothing at the interface names one.
Sampled images rest ready to sample.

An `ImageView` is an image, a format compatible with it, a range of its mip levels, a range of its layers, a dimension (`d2`, `d2Array`, `cube`, `cubeArray`, `d3`, `d1`, `d1Array`), the aspects it reads and a component mapping; `ImageView(image)` is the whole of it.
A view of a depth and stencil image that is sampled reads one aspect.
A view of a 1D image is `d1` (one layer) or `d1Array`, and one of another image's is never 1D.
The component mapping (`ComponentMapping`: `r`, `g`, `b` and `a`, each `identity`, `zero`, `one` or a letter) is how the view's four components are read by a shader: the texel's own (the identity, the default), a constant, or another of the texel's.
A mapped view is `DeviceFeatures.imageSwizzle`; a view that maps nothing (each component the identity or its own letter) is the whole image, as the image-only calls mean it, and is taken everywhere.
A mapping is for sampling: a storage view and an attachment that map components are `.invalid`, and no attachment is a 1D view.
The heap's `writeTexture` and `writeStorageImage` and a pass's attachments take a view; the calls that take an image mean the whole image, and a device that has no views (`DeviceFeatures.imageViews`) takes only those.

There is one bindless heap for each device, of five arrays: textures, samplers and storage images, and the uniform texel buffers and the storage texel buffers (below).
The caller owns the indices: it writes a slot with `writeTexture`, `writeSampler`, `writeStorageImage`, `writeTexelBuffer` or `writeStorageTexelBuffer`, and a shader names the slot.
A slot may be written again at any time; work already submitted keeps the descriptor it was submitted with.
`limits` says how many slots each array has.

## Texel buffers

A `BufferView` is a range of memory read as an array of texels of one format: the memory, an offset in it, a range in bytes (a whole number of texels; to the end of the memory where not said) and a format that is neither depth nor compressed.
A shader fetches from a uniform texel buffer (`samplerBuffer`, by `OpImageFetch`: no sampler, no filtering, no mip levels) and reads, writes and applies atomics to a storage texel buffer (`imageBuffer`).
`DeviceFeatures.uniformTexelBuffers` and `storageTexelBuffers` say whether the device has each kind, `FormatCapabilities.texelBuffer`, `storageTexelBuffer` and `storageTexelBufferAtomic` which formats, `DeviceLimits.heapTexelBuffers` and `heapStorageTexelBuffers` how many slots each array has, and `maxTexelBufferElements` the most texels a view covers.
`BufferView.check(for:limits:)` is the rule: a range that isn't whole texels, that begins before or ends past its memory, or a depth or compressed format, is `.invalid`; the features, the format's capability and the most texels are `.unsupported`.
The texel buffers are two arrays of their own in the heap, not slots among the textures and the storage images: a Vulkan device holds a buffer view and an image view in descriptors of different types, which no one array can hold, and a Mali heap's resource table has an entry for each in its own right, so a separate index space costs a device that could hold both nothing and spares the other a translation.
The arrays are bindings 3 (uniform) and 4 (storage) of the heap; an `OpImageTexelPointer` on a storage texel buffer is the atomics' address.
A device that has none keeps the default of `writeTexelBuffer` and `writeStorageTexelBuffer`, which refuse (`.unsupported`).

A sampler has a minification and a magnification filter (`filter` sets both), a mip mode, an address mode on each of the three axes (`clampToEdge`, `repeat`, `mirroredRepeat`, `mirrorClampToEdge`, `clampToBorder`; `addressMode` sets all three) and a border colour (one of six, or a `CustomBorderColor` of the sampler's own: four floats, or four integers, `DeviceFeatures.customBorderColor`, which is `.invalid` where no axis clamps to the border), a bias, a minimum and a maximum level of detail, an anisotropy, an optional comparison operation, and unnormalized coordinates.
The default sampler is the one the core had: linear, clamped, no mip levels.
A sampler that asks for what the device lacks is `.unsupported`, and one whose fields contradict each other (unnormalized coordinates with mip levels, say) is `.invalid`.

## Pipelines

`makeGraphicsPipeline` takes SPIR-V for a vertex and a fragment stage, the colour formats and the fixed state; `makeComputePipeline` takes one SPIR-V module.
The device's own compiler makes the pipeline.
Every pipeline has the one layout: the heap, and a 64-bit root pointer, which is the first eight bytes of the push constants, as a `PushConstant` block of one buffer reference.
A draw or a dispatch passes the root pointer, the GPU address of whatever the shaders read; they reach textures by the indices that data holds.
There is no vertex input state and no descriptor set to state.

A graphics pipeline's fixed state is, apart from the formats:

- `blend`, `opaque` or source alpha over, which names the blend of every target; or `attachmentBlends`, a `ColorBlendAttachment` for each (every `BlendingFactor`, a `BlendOp` for colour and for alpha, and a `ColorMask`), with `logicOp` and `blendConstants`;
- `depthStencilFormat` and `depthStencil`: the depth test, write and `CompareOp`, the depth bounds test and its bounds, and the stencil test with a `StencilFaceState` for each face (fail, pass and depth-fail `StencilOp`s, compare, masks and reference);
- `rasterization`: cull (none, front, back, both), front face, polygon mode (fill, line, point), depth bias, depth clamp, line width and rasterizer discard;
- `multisample`: a sample count, a sample mask, alpha to coverage, alpha to one and sample shading;
- `topology`: the lists and strips, `triangleFan`, `lineStrip` and `patchList`, with `primitiveRestart` and `patchControlPoints`;
- `geometry`, `tessellationControl` and `tessellationEvaluation`, the stages beyond the two, where the device has them;
- `specialization`: a value for each of the modules' specialization constants, by id;
- `viewMask`, for a multiview pass; and
- `dynamic`: which of the blend constants, the stencil reference and masks, the depth bias, the depth bounds and the line width the pass's commands set in place of the descriptor's.

Each is today's behaviour by default: no depth or stencil, no culling, filled polygons, one sample, the blend `blend` names.
A pipeline's `targets` are the attachments it draws into; a backend that says so (`GPUPipeline.targets`) has its draws held to the pass they are in.

## Command lists

A `GPUCommandList` is data: commands recorded with no device, and replayed by the backend at `submit`.
It has compute dispatches (direct and indirect), passes with dynamic rendering, draws (direct, indexed, indirect, and indirect with a count), copies, clears, blits and resolves, barriers, and queries.

A pass is `beginRendering` with the attachments, each saying what happens to its contents at the pass's start (`load`, `clear` or `dontCare`) and end (`store` or `discard`), as a tiler needs before the pass begins; `endRendering`; and the draws between.
`RenderingInfo` has the colour attachments, each a view, with an optional single-sample view to resolve into; an optional depth and stencil attachment with a load and a store for each aspect and its own clear value; a layer count; a view mask for multiview; and a render area.
Every viewport and scissor starts as the whole of the first attachment, with depths 0 to 1.
`setViewport` and `setScissor` set the first; `setViewports(first:)` sets a run of viewports, each a rectangle (a negative height flips it) with its depth range, and `setScissors(first:)` a run of scissors, one for each viewport.
A pipeline draws with `viewportCount` of them (1 by default), and a primitive takes the one its ViewportIndex names; more than one is `multiViewport`, up to `maxViewports`.
A viewport of no width or height, depths outside 0 to 1, a scissor before the origin, and none at all are `.invalid`; one past the device's count, size or bounds is `.unsupported`.

The copies are: memory to memory; memory to an image and an image to memory, whole (mip level 0 of layer 0, rows tightly packed) or by regions, each with a mip level, layers, an offset, an extent, a buffer offset and row length; image to image (`copyImage`, between formats of one class); `blitImage`, which scales regions with a nearest or linear filter and flips where the corners are swapped; `resolveImage`, from a multisampled image into a single-sampled one of its format; `fill` and `update` of memory.
A region lies inside its subresource, and where the format is compressed it starts on a block and ends on one or on the image's edge.
`clear` an image (a colour, whose type is the format's: float, unsigned or signed; or depth and stencil) and `clearAttachments` inside a pass.
Inside a pass, `setBlendConstants`, `setStencilReference`, `setStencilCompareMask`, `setStencilWriteMask`, `setDepthBias`, `setDepthBounds` and `setLineWidth` set the state a pipeline's `dynamic` leaves to them.
`drawIndirectCount` and `drawIndexedIndirectCount` take the number of draws from memory, up to a maximum.

A barrier names stages only (`compute`, `vertex`, `fragment`, `transfer`): the earlier stages' writes are visible to the later stages' reads and writes.
A barrier that names an image and a range of its subresources is accepted, for the backends that can use the range; a device that can't treats it as the barrier without it.

`validate` is the one place the list's rules are written, and every backend holds a list to it before it runs.
They include the old ones (a draw outside a pass, a dispatch inside one, a copy past its memory, a copy into an image that isn't a copy destination, a pass that never ends) and these:

- an attachment is a render target (or a depth and stencil target, of a depth or stencil format); all the attachments are of one size and one sample count; a view of an attachment is one mip level; a resolve target matches its attachment's format and size and has one sample; a multiview pass has one layer count; the render area is inside the attachments;
- a draw's pipeline, where it says what it draws into, matches the pass;
- a copy, blit, resolve or clear names a usage the image has, regions inside the image, formats that are compatible, a sample count that fits, and an aspect the format has; a clear colour has the type of the format;
- a state command is inside a pass, and its values are in range; an indirect draw's arguments and count are inside their memory and aligned;
- a query is of the kind its set is, and its index is in the set.

A list that breaks them is `.invalid`; a list that needs what the device can't do is `.unsupported`.

## Features and limits

`DeviceLimits.features` has a flag for each optional capability, all false until a backend says otherwise:

- images: `depth`, `stencil`, `mipmaps`, `arrayImages`, `cubeImages`, `volumeImages`, `images1D`, `multisample`, `compressedASTC`, `compressedETC2`, `compressedBC`, `imageViews`, `imageSwizzle`;
- texel buffers: `uniformTexelBuffers`, `storageTexelBuffers`;
- samplers: `samplerAnisotropy`, `samplerCompare`, `samplerBorder`, `unnormalizedCoordinates`, `samplerMinMagFilters`, `samplerAxisAddressModes`, `samplerMirrorClampToEdge`, `customBorderColor`;
- pipeline state: `cullModes`, `rasterizerDiscard`, `independentBlend`, `dualSourceBlend`, `logicOps`, `fillModeNonSolid`, `wideLines`, `depthClamp`, `depthBias`, `depthBounds`, `geometryShader`, `tessellation`, `multiview`, `extendedTopologies`, `primitiveRestart`, `sampleShading`, `alphaToCoverage`, `alphaToOne`, `specializationConstants`, `dynamicState`, `multipleColorAttachments`;
- commands: `copyImage`, `blitImage`, `resolveImage`, `resolveAttachments`, `depthStencilResolve`, `imageRegionCopies`, `fillBuffer`, `updateBuffer`, `clearImage`, `clearAttachments`, `drawIndirectCount`, `pipelineStatisticsQueries`, `importHandle`, `presentDirect`;
- what shaders may do (SPIR-V's capabilities): `shaderFloat16`, `shaderInt8`, `shaderInt16`, `shaderInt64`, `shaderFloat64`, `subgroupOperations`, `shaderAtomic32`, `shaderAtomic64`, `shaderImageAtomics`, `shaderDerivatives`, the storage image formats, clip and cull distances, gather, minimum level of detail, draw parameters, demote, layer and viewport index, stencil export, sample-rate shading and float controls;
- viewports: `multiViewport`, and `shaderOutputViewportIndex` (ViewportIndex from the vertex stage; `shaderViewportIndexLayer` is Layer from it);
- sparse resources: `sparseBinding`, `sparseResidencyBuffer`, `sparseResidencyImage2D`, `sparseResidencyImage3D`, `sparseResidency2Samples` to `sparseResidency16Samples`, `sparseResidencyAliased`, `shaderResourceResidency`;
- the ones the core had: `computeQueue`, `indirectDispatch`, `indirectDraw`, `indexedDraw`, `sampledImages`, `storageImages`, `timestampQueries`, `occlusionQueries`, `loadAttachments`, `multipleDrawsPerPass`.

The limits are the heap's slots, `maxImageDimension` (and for 3D, `maxImageDimension3D`), `maxImageArrayLayers`, `maxSamples`, `maxAnisotropy`, `maxLineWidth`, `maxColorAttachments`, `maxMultiviewViews`, `maxPatchControlPoints`, `subgroupSize`, the timestamp period, `maxViewports` with the largest viewport, the bounds its corners lie in and its sub-pixel bits, and `sparseAddressSpaceSize` with `sparse`, how the device lays out and reads sparse images (the standard block shapes, aligned mip sizes, and whether unbound pages read as zero).

## Sparse resources

A sparse buffer (`makeSparseBuffer`) is `.device` memory with an address and no pages, which a list uses like any other; a sparse image is one whose descriptor's `sparse` is not empty, made with no memory.
Their pages are bound by the queue's `bindSparse`, which takes ranges of buffers (`SparseBufferBind`), ranges of an image's opaque bytes (`SparseImageOpaqueBind`, its metadata too where the device has any) and, for an image used partly bound, boxes of whole blocks of one level and layer (`SparseImageBind`), each to a range of ordinary memory or to nothing.
`bindSparse` waits for `wait` and for everything submitted on the queue before it, and signals `signal` on the timeline, and the queue's later submissions run after it; `TimelineRule` holds its values as `submit`'s.
A resource made with `.binding` is wholly bound before work uses it; with `.residency` (and the device's residency feature for the resource's kind and samples) work may use it partly bound, and what is unbound reads as zero where `sparse.residencyNonResidentStrict` says so, and drops writes; with `.aliased` a page may be bound at more than one place.
`sparseRequirements` gives a resource's range in whole pages and the page every bind's offsets and sizes are a multiple of, in the resource and in the memory; `sparseImageRequirements` gives each aspect's block of texels and mip tail (the levels bound by bytes, not blocks); `sparseImageFormats` gives the blocks of a format, kind, sample count and usage before an image is made.
`SparseBindings.validate` judges the binds' shape (a resource that isn't sparse, memory that is, a range past either, blocks past a level or of an image without residency are `.invalid`); a bind off the pages, or of memory of a kind the resource can't take, is the backend's to refuse.
The shape is one every GPU with page tables has: a bind is a run of the memory's pages put at the resource's addresses, and a range bound to nothing is unmapped or mapped to a page of zeros.
A device without them keeps the defaults, which refuse with `.unsupported`.

## The timeline

A device has one timeline: a 64-bit counter that only goes up, which the GPU class's counters are (counter(2)).
`submit` waits until the timeline reaches `wait` (0 for no wait) and sets it to `signal` when the list is done; `signal` must be greater than any value the device has signalled, and `wait` must be a value some earlier submission signalled (`TimelineRule` keeps these).
Lists on one queue run in the order submitted.
`completed` is the highest value reached and `wait(_:)` waits for one.
A `.readback` allocation's bytes are the CPU's to read once the timeline has reached the value the submission that wrote them signalled.
`timelineHandle()` gives a new handle to a kernel counter that tracks the timeline (it reads at least a value once the timeline has reached it), which its holder may read and wait on but not add to; a window's images take it as their acquire counter (below). It is nil where the device's timeline is no such counter: under Venus it is a timeline semaphore of the host's.

## Queries

`makeQuerySet` makes a set of timestamps (in ticks of `limits.timestampPeriodPicoseconds`), of occlusion counts, or of pipeline statistics (the counters `PipelineStatistics` names); `writeTimestamp` and `beginQuery` and `endQuery` fill it and `results` reads it after the work has completed.
A statistics set's results hold one value for each counter of each query, in the order of the counters' bits.

## The Vulkan backend

`VulkanDevice` is Loinnir's Vulkan code behind the core: one device with dynamic rendering, buffer device addresses, descriptor indexing and timeline semaphores, and the layout transitions a list needs, made as it replays the list into a command buffer.
Memory is a buffer with a device address; the heap is one descriptor set (sampled images at binding 0, samplers at binding 1, 16 of them, and with `storageImages: true`, where the device offers the features, storage images at binding 2, and with `texelBuffers: true`, where it offers its update-after-bind and non-uniform indexing features, 1024 uniform texel buffers at binding 3 and 1024 storage texel buffers at binding 4).
It implements the whole of the core, as far as the device under it does: each `DeviceFeatures` flag and each `DeviceLimits` value is read from what the physical device reports, at start, and nothing is assumed.
The features the core names are asked of the device (the 1.0 set, and the 1.1, 1.2 and 1.3 sets) and the ones it has are enabled; the properties give the sizes and the sample counts.
A flag for what Vulkan 1.3 requires of every device (the backend's device needs 1.3's dynamic rendering) is set when the device reports 1.3 or later, and one for an optional feature when the device has it: independent and dual-source blending, logic operations, the non-fill polygon modes, wide lines, depth clamp, depth bounds, geometry and tessellation shaders, sample shading, anisotropy, the compressed families (ASTC, ETC2, BC), multiview, counted draws, pipeline statistics, the 8-, 16- and 64-bit shader types, 64-bit atomics, and the shader capabilities the rest of the flags name.
`depth` and `stencil` are set when some depth or stencil format is a depth target, `multisample` when the device has colour attachments of more than one sample, `arrayImages` and `volumeImages` from the limits of layers and of a 3D side, and the transfer commands (image copies, blits, resolves, regions, fills, updates, clears) when the queue the backend uses does graphics.
`alphaToOne`, `depthStencilResolve` and `importHandle` are false: the backend doesn't enable alpha to one or resolve a depth attachment, and Venus implements only the exporting side of memory sharing (`exportHandle` works), so an import is `.unsupported`.
`formats(_:)` is filled from the device's own format properties and sample counts, one entry for each format the device can sample, draw into, store to, blit or use as a depth target.
A list is replayed into a command buffer with each image's layout tracked from the commands that use it.
A pass is a dynamic rendering pass, with a depth and stencil attachment, a resolve for each colour attachment, layers and a view mask.
The pipeline state of the core (depth and stencil, rasterization, multisampling, blending for each attachment and logic operations, the dynamic states, the topologies, the optional stages and specialization constants) is the pipeline's, one to one; a specialization constant is 32 bits, or 64 where its value has bits past the 32nd.
A heap write of a view keeps a view of the image (a format, a range of mip levels and layers, a dimension: 1D, 1D array, 2D, array, cube, cube array or 3D, and the component mapping, one to one), made once and cached, and a sampler is the heap's own, one to one.
A 1D image is a 1D Vulkan image, and a heap write of a `BufferView` makes a `VkBufferView` of the allocation's buffer at the offset, range and format, kept until the allocation is freed (the offset must be a multiple of the device's `minTexelBufferOffsetAlignment`, which a view made at another is refused for, `.unsupported`); every allocation can be a texel buffer.
A sampler's own border colour is `VK_EXT_custom_border_color`'s, with no format named, taken where the host's Vulkan has the extension and both of its features; `customBorderColor` is set from them.
Under Venus the extension is the renderer's, which Venus's driver forwards where the host's device offers it and the renderer carries it (loinnir(2)); the texel-buffer features the heap needs (update after bind and non-uniform indexing of the two kinds) and the 1D and mapped views are whatever the host reports.
More than one viewport is the device's `multiViewport` and `maxViewports`, and every one of them is set at the start of a pass; a pipeline's viewport state has its `viewportCount`.
Sparse resources are the device's sparse features, where the queue the backend uses has `VK_QUEUE_SPARSE_BINDING_BIT`: a sparse buffer or image is made with the sparse flags and no memory, `bindSparse` is `vkQueueBindSparse` of the `VkDeviceMemory` behind the memory named (whose type the resource must take), waiting on the timeline semaphore for everything submitted before it, and the next submission waits for the bind; `shaderResourceResidency` is false.
The queries are occlusion, timestamp and pipeline statistics, and `validate` judges a list against the device's limits before it is replayed.
Everything the device lacks it refuses at the call that makes the image, the sampler or the pipeline, or at `submit`, with `.unsupported`.
loinnir-test(8) prints which flags the device behind Venus reports, and draws, copies and judges each of these against a CPU reference.
On the dev machine it opens the host's Vulkan, or ours over Venus and vtest when `TODHCHAI_VULKAN_LIBRARY` names `libtdvulkan.so`.

## The Mali backend

`MaliDevice` runs on one VM of a `csf` context: each allocation is a buffer bound at a page-aligned address, and a list becomes steps.
A compute dispatch and a render pass of its draws are MaliCore's recipes (ComputeDispatch and RenderPass) laid out in memory of the VM, with the pipelines compiled from SPIR-V when they are made (compute shaders may not name descriptor buffers: the core's layout is the heap and a root pointer).
A copy, blit, clear, fill, update or resolve, and a pass with no draw, is a compute dispatch of the backend's own kernels, in order with the passes and dispatches (see the transfers below); the CPU touches no pixel of an image.
A submission is one submit to the driver: its steps are streams on the queues of one group (queue 0 the vertex and tiler work, queue 1 the fragment work, queue 2 the dispatches, the transfers' kernels and the timestamps), each step's streams waiting on the GPU (SYNC_WAIT64) for a 64-bit sync object of the device's to pass the step before, and each step adding one to it when it is done (SYNC_ADD64, after its tail's clean): the CPU waits for nothing between the steps, and a list of several passes, dispatches and transfers costs one round trip. A stream on the queue the step before ended on doesn't wait (the queue's order is the wait, and each job starts with the driver's clean and invalidate): two kernels in a row, or a pass's fragment stream after a pass; a wait between queues costs the GPU some 18 us on the Orange Pi 6 Plus.
After the wait, a step's streams clean and invalidate the shader cores' caches (the LSC, the texture unit's and the tile readers'), so it reads what the step before wrote; the L2 stays.
A submission is laid out while the one before it runs, and waits for the one before that: at most two are on the GPU.
What a submission lays out (its steps' blocks and streams, a transfer's parameters, the frame shaders' heaps, thread and workgroup storage) is carved from chunks of 4 MiB, made and bound once in one 4 GiB window and used again once the timeline has passed every submission that used them: a frame loop makes no buffer and calls the driver once a frame.
A fault that ends the group is found when the submission's value is waited for (the steps that never ran added nothing to the sync object), and `wait` throws.
`MaliTransport` is what it needs of the GPU's streams interface; `CSFTransport` is that interface over the `csf` capset's client, and a test can give another.
What it can do now: memory by intent; linear `rgba8`, `bgra8`, `bgra8srgb` and `r8` images, which a render pass writes and the texture unit reads in the same layout; the heap's textures, samplers and storage images; compute pipelines and dispatches, direct and indirect; graphics pipelines for `rgba8`, `bgra8` or `bgra8srgb` targets, and passes of any number of draws, direct, indexed, from a first vertex or instance, and indirect (a draw count of the command, its arguments read from memory by the stream when it runs), whose attachment is cleared, `dontCare` or kept at its start; timestamp queries.
An indexed draw gives the tiler the index data's address and size, its type (`uint16` or `uint32`), the first index and the base vertex (signed, added to each index); the vertex shader's `VertexIndex` is the index plus the base vertex, and a draw from a first vertex has the first vertex in it.
An indirect draw's four words (vertex count, instance count, first vertex, first instance), or an indexed one's five, are loaded into the run's count registers by the stream, which waits for the load before it issues the run; each of `drawCount` draws is its own run, `stride` bytes on.
A draw whose vertex or index count or instance count is zero is not issued: the stream branches over the run when the register is zero, since what the hardware does with a zero count in a run is not stated.
An indirect dispatch loads its three words into the run's grid registers (`JOB_SIZE_X/Y/Z`) and into the shader's number-of-workgroups words, and issues RUN_COMPUTE on the X axis with the largest increment a task holds, as the hardware's indirect form is not used; workgroup memory is sized for the largest grid a core can hold.
A dispatch with a zero in any of the three words is not issued either (three branches over the run); the shader never runs and the stream goes on to the next step.
A pass that keeps its attachment (`load`) has a first pre-frame shader, run once for each tile before the pass's draws: it fetches the target's texel at the pixel and writes it as the colour, in a heap of its own made for the pass (the nearest sampler and the target as a texture), and the pass's opaque blend stores it in the tile as it is; a second pass over an image keeps what the first drew.
A pass that keeps its attachment and draws nothing is no work.
A timestamp is the system timer's counter stored by a stream of the compute queue once every step before it has finished, so the stages a `writeTimestamp` names make no difference.
The GPU's counter runs at the frequency of the CPU's architected counter, so the tick is the inverse of what `CNTFRQ_EL0` reads (`MaliTransport.counterFrequency`; 1000 ps on the Orange Pi 6 Plus, which two stores 40 ms apart against the system clock also measure); only where that register reads 0 does the first read of `limits` measure it, with two small streams, and a GPU whose counter can't be read then has no timestamp queries.
An occlusion query is the draws' 64-bit counter: a draw's counter mode (draw flags 0 `[15:14]` 3) and the query's address in the run's `OQ` register make the hardware add the samples that passed into it, in its caches.
The memory of an occlusion query set is therefore mapped cacheable for the GPU (`MaliTransport.bind`'s `gpuCacheable`; in write-combining memory the count stays 0).
The pass's fragment queue stores a zero over each report that the pass begins a query on, before its fragment work and followed by a clean flush; its tail's clean flush, which waits for the fragment work, puts the counts in memory before the submission signals, so the CPU reads them with no maintenance.
A query begun in a pass counts the samples of the draws between `beginQuery` and `endQuery` that pass the tests, and a pass with no draw zeroes its queries with a fill, in order with the rest.
A storage image is a texture descriptor of one level with the identity swizzle over a generic linear plane, in the storage array that is table 2 of the resource table (1024 slots, apart from the textures' and samplers'); the shaders' handle for element 0 of binding 2 of set 0 is table 2, index 0, and a dispatch or a pass whose shaders name a storage image is given a copy of the array as it stands.
`rgba8`, `bgra8` and `r8` have storage (no atomics); `bgra8srgb` has none; `r8uint`, `r8sint`, `r16uint` and `r16sint` are storage images only; `r32uint` and `r32sint` are storage images with atomics (`storageAtomic`, `shaderImageAtomics`).
Out of range values stored to an 8 or 16-bit integer storage image saturate, and a store, an atomic or a load out of bounds reads zeros (alpha 1) and changes no memory.
A texel buffer is a Buffer descriptor (type 9, buffer type 3: the view's address, its range in bytes, the texel's size as the stride, and in word 7 the format's 22-bit pixel format word) in the same table 2, after the storage images: slots 1024 to 2047 hold the uniform texel buffers (the heap's binding 3) and 2048 to 3071 the storage ones (binding 4), so the shaders' handle for element 0 of binding 3 is table 2, index 1024, and of binding 4 index 2048.
The formats with texel buffers (both kinds) are the one to four-component 8, 16 and 32-bit normalised, integer and float formats the specification's table names, `rgb10a2unorm`, `rg11b10float` and `rgb9e5`, and `bgra8`; `rgba8srgb` and `bgra8srgb` have none.
`r32uint` and `r32sint` have `storageTexelBufferAtomic`.
A fetch of a format that has fewer than four channels reads (R, 0, 0, 1) or (R, G, 0, 1), as the board shows the hardware fills them, and a view never written reads zeros.
The heap has 4096 texture slots and 16 sampler slots.
`writeTexture` and `writeSampler` keep MaliCore's descriptors (malicore(2)) in the device's copy of the heap, the samplers first and then the textures, and a pass whose fragment shaders read the heap is given a copy of it as it stands when the pass is built, as table 1 of its resource table: a later write reaches later passes only. The copy is made again only when a slot was written since the last (so a program that writes its textures once, and not each frame, copies nothing).
A sampler has the filter (the same for magnifying and minifying), the address mode on all three axes (`clampToEdge`, `repeat` or `mirroredRepeat`), no mipmaps and a transparent black border.
The vertex and fragment stages and compute shaders read the heap (a vertex shader at the level it names, its implicit level the base), with a texture array at binding 0 and a sampler array at binding 1 of set 0, as Loinnir's shaders do (a dispatch is given a copy of it as it stands, as a pass is, and a compute shader reads a level it names: no derivatives).
A view is a texture descriptor over an array of plane descriptors, one for each layer and level it covers; a view of a whole colour image uses the array the image keeps after its texels, any other has an array of its own, held until its slot is written again.
A view's component mapping is the descriptor's swizzle (valhall 03 section 3.8.1).
A pipeline's blend is each target's own (`independentBlend`): the fixed-function stage does an equation of one multiplier, and a blend shader the compiler makes does the rest (minimum and maximum, the saturated source alpha, constants that differ between channels, dual-source blending, logic operations); the blend constants may be a command's (`DynamicStates.blendConstants`), and a draw's constants decide which of the two a target uses.
On a `bgra8srgb` target the stage decodes the destination, blends in light and encodes the result, and a clear is encoded too; the fragment shader's colour is light.
A draw of no instances, or of fewer vertices than its topology's primitive has, makes nothing.
The topologies are the triangle list, strip and fan, the line list and strip and the point list (`extendedTopologies`), with primitive restart on the strips and fans (`primitiveRestart`, the all-ones index of the index type).
A point is one pixel, and a line is its width wide (`wideLines`, up to 7.9921875), which the pipeline fixes or the pass sets (`DynamicStates.lineWidth`).
Culling is front, back or both for the triangle topologies, whichever way the front face is wound (`cullModes`); a rasterizer-discard pipeline's draws are not run (`rasterizerDiscard`).
The viewport and scissor are any box of the target: the viewport is the vertex stage's window transform and the hardware's viewport rectangle, the scissor the draw's scissor box, and a pass starts with the whole target for both.
It has one viewport and scissor (`multiViewport` and `shaderOutputViewportIndex` are false, `maxViewports` 1): `setViewports` and `setScissors` of the first take it, with the viewport's depth range, and more is `.unsupported`. A vertex shader that writes ViewportIndex anyway takes its transform from the pass's viewports by the index (the transform is the shader's on this hardware), and the first scissor.
Sparse resources are `sparseBinding`, `sparseResidencyBuffer`, `sparseResidencyImage2D` and `sparseResidencyImage3D`; not multisampled residency, aliasing or `shaderResourceResidency`. A sparse buffer or image is a range of the GPU's addresses reserved when it is made (aligned to 2 MiB from 2 MiB on); a bind maps a range of a memory's pages there, and a bind to nothing maps the range to the driver's dummy, 2 MiB that every such range of the process repeats, never leaves it unmapped (an access to an unmapped page is a fault that ends the work). A resource used partly bound is mapped to the dummy over its whole range when it is made; one bound only as a whole is unmapped until it is bound, and must be bound before work uses it. What is unbound reads what was last written through any unbound range, not zero (`residencyNonResidentStrict` is false), and takes writes. Buffers, and images bound only as a whole (which are linear), are bound by 4 KiB pages. An image used partly bound is in the GPU's 64 KiB layout: tiles of 64 KiB, 256 x 256 texels of 1 byte down to 64 x 64 of 16, one slice deep in a volume (`residencyStandard2DBlockShape`, not the standard 3D shape), each tile a block; every level is whole tiles, so the mip tail is the last level of each layer (a one-level image is all tail). Such an image is 2D, cube or 3D, of one sample and one aspect, a texel of 1 to 16 bytes and not compressed, and is sampled, stored to, copied (the transfer kernels address its tiles) and drawn into as a colour target, never a depth or stencil target. A bind runs as a submission does: after everything submitted before it has finished, and the timeline reaches its signal when it is done.
A pass's depth and stencil attachment is a `depth16`, `depth32f`, `stencil8`, `depth24stencil8` or `depth32fstencil8` image (the last two a depth plane, D24X8 or D32, and a stencil plane, S8: valhall 08 section 8.5.1) (`depth`, `stencil`: a plane of its own, linear, made only for the `depthStencilTarget` usage), cleared, kept or not cared about at the start and stored or discarded at the end.
A pass that keeps a depth or stencil plane has a second pre-frame shader (valhall 08 section 8.2.1.1; Early ZS Always), run once for each tile before the draws: it fetches the plane at the pixel (each sample, where the pass is multisampled) and writes it as the fragment's depth, or gives it as the stencil reference with the test Always and every operation Replace, the tests late; the planes' preload bits are never set.
The depth test has every compare op, the depth write, depth bias with its clamp (the constant factor is the API's, as the hardware has it) and depth clamp (`depthBias`, `depthClamp`); the stencil test has every operation, the compare and write masks and the reference of each face, the faces being the winding's, and the masks, reference and bias may be a pass's (`DynamicStates`, `dynamicState`).
The depth and stencil tests update early or late as the fragment shader needs: a shader that writes the depth, or can discard while the state writes depth or stencil, updates late, so a discarded fragment leaves the depth alone.
A pass has up to eight colour targets (`multipleColorAttachments`, `maxColorAttachments`) of one size, each cleared or not cared about; a target the fragment shader doesn't write is not blended and keeps its clear.
`clearAttachments` inside a pass is a draw of the pass, in order with its others: a rectangle over the whole target is drawn with the cleared rectangle as its scissor, a colour attachment's through a fragment shader of its location that writes the clear colour with an opaque blend (every other target off), the depth and stencil in one draw whose rectangle lies at the depth cleared, the depth test Always and written, the stencil test Always and Replace with the value cleared, and no colour; the pass's own viewport, scissor and state are not used.
A shader that writes the stencil reference (`FragStencilRefEXT`, ZS_EMIT's stencil) gives it to the stencil test where the test is on (the depth/stencil descriptor's stencil from the shader).

Multisampling (`multisample`) has 2, 4, 8 and 16 samples (`maxSamples` 16), the hardware's sample patterns (valhall 07 section 7.9: rotated 2x and 4x, D3D 8x and 16x), which are Vulkan's standard locations: the `rgba8`, `bgra8` and `bgra8srgb` targets and the `depth16`, `depth32f` and `stencil8` attachments take every count, and are sampled (a fetch of a sample) at every count.
A multisampled image has a plane of each sample, a surface apart, in each layer (valhall 05 section 5.5.3); the tile holds every sample (the tile size shrinks with the count, 08 section 8.8).
A stored target writes each sample to its surface (writeback Layered); a target resolved as the pass ends (`resolveAttachments`) and not stored writes the mean of its samples to the resolve image (writeback Average); one both stored and resolved has a second render-target descriptor over the same tile, the mean to the resolve image.
A multisampled depth and stencil plane is stored a sample to its surface; one resolved (`depthStencilResolve`) takes sample 0: written by the writeback (Single) into the resolve image where it isn't stored, and copied by the copy kernel from the stored samples after the pass where it is.
A pass that keeps a multisampled target loads it a sample at a time (the pre-frame shader run per sample).
`resolveImage` is the copy kernel: each byte of a texel the mean of its samples' bytes, rounded to nearest, as the writeback averages them.
A draw of a multisampled pass rasterizes at the samples (draw flags 0 `[19]`); the pipeline's sample mask is the draw's (flags 1); sample shading (`sampleShading`) is per sample whatever the fraction above 0 (flags 0 `[11]`; the hardware has no fraction), as is a shader that reads `SampleId` or `SamplePosition`, or a draw that calls a blend shader; alpha to coverage (`alphaToCoverage`) is the hardware's mapping of colour 0's alpha (flags 0 `[22]`; alpha 0.5 covers two of four samples), and alpha to one (`alphaToOne`) a blend shader that writes the alpha as 1 before it blends.
An attachment is its view: one level of one or more layers, drawn at the level's size.
A pass of layers (`layerCount`) has a framebuffer descriptor group and a fragment run for each layer, the layer's index in its tiler context and the layer itself as the frame argument, and a tiler context for each eight layers (valhall 07 section 7.8); a draw whose vertex shader writes Layer (`shaderViewportIndexLayer`) is issued once for each context, with the layer index enabled and the extended position record, and one that doesn't goes to the first layer.
A kept attachment of a pass of layers is loaded at each layer by the frame shaders, which read the layer they run for (the frame argument) from an array of the pass's layers; `clearAttachments` of layers draws an instance a layer.
Multiview (`multiview`, `maxMultiviewViews` 8; 07 section 7.8.2): a pipeline's vertex shader is compiled once for all the views of its mask, its body replicated for each view with ViewIndex that view's constant, and each view's outputs stored at the view's bit position's slots; a multiview pass has one tiler context whose draws name the mask, and a descriptor group and a fragment run for each view in the mask, the view its layer and its fragment shaders' ViewIndex; a clear inside it is cleared in every view, and a kept attachment is loaded at each view's layer.
There are no polygon modes (`fillModeNonSolid`): the hardware draws triangles, lines and points as they are, with no wireframe or point fill (valhall 06 section 6.4.6), as the reference does.



Images, views and samplers are the resources of the texture unit (valhall 03 section 3.7 to 3.9 and 05):
- An image is linear in memory the CPU maps, but for an AFBC one (valhall 05 section 5.11): a 2D target of one level, layer and sample, at least 16 x 16, of `rgba8`, `bgra8`, `bgra8srgb`, `depth16` or `depth32f`, whose usage is no more than `renderTarget`, `depthStencilTarget` and `sampled` (nothing copies it, clears it with a kernel, stores to it or maps it). Its level is a header area of 16 bytes for each 16 x 16 superblock, linear headers, and the sparse body after it at 128 bytes; a pass writes it compressed (the colour transform on for colour), a superblock the clear fills is one header and no body, and the texture unit reads it (the AFBC plane, mode R8G8B8A8, R8G8 or R8). A pass over it that draws nothing draws its clears. `MaliDevice.afbc` turns it off for images made after.
- A colour target (one sample, not a storage image or a window's) has a CRC table for its level 0 (valhall 08 section 8.6, transaction elimination): a pass over the whole of level 0, of one layer and sample, with an effective tile of 16 x 16 or more, checks each tile's checksum and writes back only the tiles that changed, and an empty tile's checksum is the clears' signature. A write by anything else (a kernel, a pass that doesn't check it) changes the table's counter, so no checksum stored before matches. `MaliDevice.crcTables` turns it off for images made after.
- A sampled colour image (2D or cube, one sample, not compressed, not a storage image or a window's) that isn't AFBC is in 64 KiB tiles of 16 x 16 u-curve blocks (valhall 05 section 5.7, the layout of a sparse image used partly bound), so a sampler's neighbouring texels share cache lines both ways; the transfer kernels address its tiles and a pass writes them (block format 8). `MaliDevice.tiledTextures` turns it off for images made after.
- Other than that, every image is linear in memory the CPU maps. It has one plane for a colour format and one for each aspect of a depth or stencil format (the depth plane first), each holding all the image's mip levels, layers and slices in the layout of 05 sections 5.5 and 5.5.4: rows and levels aligned to 64 bytes, a compressed format's rows in blocks, a cube's faces six consecutive layers, a volume's slices a surface apart.
- A texture or storage slot holds a texture descriptor over an array of plane descriptors, one for each layer and level the view covers; the whole image of a colour format uses the array the image keeps after its texels, any other view has an array of its own, held until its slot is written again.
- A view of another format reads the same bytes: the format's word is the view's, the plane's clump format follows its size, and the sRGB bit is the view's. A depth or stencil view reads its one aspect as (value, 0, 0, 1), the component mapping composed with it.
- A block-compressed view sets the descriptor's texel interleave bit, which the texture unit needs for ASTC, ETC2 and BC alike.
- A sampler is the descriptor's filters, addressing per axis (`mirrorClampToEdge` is mirrored clamp to edge), border (the standard colours or the sampler's own four words), mipmap mode, level-of-detail bias and range in 8.8 fixed point, anisotropy (the anisotropic algorithm with the maximum given), comparison (the function mirrored, as the hardware compares the texel with the reference) and unnormalized coordinates.
- A compute dispatch is given the heap as a pass is: the textures and samplers as table 1, the storage images and texel buffers as table 2; the dispatch's FAU block holds the handles of its bindings and a dummy sampler for the fetches of plain images.

Copies, blits, clears, fills, updates and resolves run on the GPU, as compute dispatches in order with the list's passes and dispatches, with no wait of the CPU's and no cache maintenance between them (there is no copy engine; an AFBC image is never a transfer's).
Each command is one dispatch of all its regions and aspects (a blit that both moves bytes and converts is two); its parameters, and the bytes a fill or an update writes (an update's data, up to 64 KiB, staged in memory of the submission's), are in memory the backend holds until the submission is done.
- `copy` moves rows of 1, 2, 4, 8 or 16-byte units through memory accesses, the largest unit the addresses and strides allow: memory to memory, buffers and images both ways (any row length and image height, mip level, layers and slices of a volume), `copyImage` (whole blocks, any format of the same size, a compressed one block by block, a depth or stencil aspect as its plane's bytes, a multisampled image sample by sample), `fill` and `update`, `clearImage` and the clear of a depth and stencil image over subresource ranges (the texel's bytes, made once from the value: unorm, snorm, sRGB, float, the integers, rgb10a2, rg11b10f and rgb9e5; a depth and stencil image's depth and stencil), the clears of a pass with no draw, and `resolveImage` (each byte the mean of the samples' bytes, rounded).
- `blitRaw` does a blit of one format with the nearest filter, a depth and stencil one included (both blits' only form): each texel's bytes from the source texel nearest its centre, scaled and flipped as the corners say.
- `blit` does any other: the texture unit reads the source, with the blit's filter and the format's decoding (sRGB in light), from a heap of the dispatch's own (a nearest and a linear sampler, clamped to the edge, and the source's level as a 2D array or a 3D texture; an integer volume as an array of its slices, fetched), and the kernel stores each texel in the destination's format (to nearest; integers saturated to the narrower format).

## Memory and the GPU's caches

The GPU maps a client's memory (every intent, and imported memory), a storage image's and an occlusion query set's write-back cacheable (the bind's attribute index 1), where shader atomics (`shaderAtomic32`) and the occlusion counters work; other images, the streams, the shaders' code and the heap's copies are write-combining for it. The CPU's mapping is write-combining but for readback memory and images the CPU only reads, which it caches and maintains.
A submission's last step ends by waiting on every scoreboard slot, then cleaning the L2 and the LSC with a flush id of zero loaded just before, and waiting for the clean: what the GPU wrote is in memory before the submission signals. A step before it only drains its slots (the next step reads through the L2, after its own clean and invalidate of the shader cores' caches), and a pass's tiling stream never cleans (its fragment stream does).
A submission's streams of one queue are one job, a stream that CALLs each in order; a wait for the value last submitted watches the chain's word in memory for up to 1 ms before it waits on the timeline, so it returns when the GPU is done rather than when the driver's interrupt says so (`MaliDevice.mergesQueueJobs`, `spinsForCompletion`).
Every pass points at one table of the sample patterns, which the device keeps. Every job begins with the driver's clean and invalidate of the GPU's caches, so the GPU reads what the CPU wrote between submissions.

Every linear image the backend lays out has rows of `width` times the texel's bytes rounded up to 64, render targets and `scanout` images included, as valhall 05 §5.5.5 (R1.1) has a linear plane's row stride: a multiple of 64 bytes, or the texture unit reads zeros.
`exportHandle(_ memory:)` gives a new handle to the VMO behind memory, which another process may map and duplicate; `exportWindowHandle(_ image:)` the same for a `scanout` image, with its row stride; `exportHandle(_ image:)` gives the display's, which can't map, and only where the rows are `width * 4` bytes.
`importHandle` maps the pages of such a handle into the device's VM (memory cacheable for the GPU, as a client's memory is; an image write-combining, as the owner's `scanout` image is), and as the owner mapped them for the CPU; an imported image is one level of four-byte texels from the start of the VMO in rows `rowBytes` apart, which must be a multiple of 64 and at least the width's bytes, else the import is `.unsupported`, with no copy to fall back on; its views have arrays of their own.
`timelineHandle()` is a new handle to the timeline's sync, a counter of the client's that the driver raises, with the rights to read and wait on it.

Its `DeviceFeatures` are `sampledImages`, `multipleDrawsPerPass`, `indexedDraw`, `indirectDraw`, `indirectDispatch`, `loadAttachments`, `storageImages`, `shaderAtomic32`, `shaderImageAtomics`, `uniformTexelBuffers`, `storageTexelBuffers`, `timestampQueries`, `occlusionQueries`, `imageViews`, `imageSwizzle`, `images1D`, `mipmaps`, `arrayImages`, `cubeImages`, `volumeImages`, `depth`, `stencil`, `compressedASTC`, `compressedETC2` and `compressedBC` (each of the three compressed families where the GPU's TEXTURE_FEATURES[0] has every bit of it), `samplerAnisotropy`, `samplerCompare`, `samplerBorder`, `customBorderColor`, `unnormalizedCoordinates`, `samplerAxisAddressModes`, `samplerMirrorClampToEdge`, `copyImage`, `blitImage`, `imageRegionCopies`, `fillBuffer`, `updateBuffer`, `clearImage`, `clearAttachments`, `multisample`, `resolveAttachments`, `resolveImage`, `depthStencilResolve`, `shaderViewportIndexLayer`, `multiview`, `sparseBinding`, `sparseResidencyBuffer`, `sparseResidencyImage2D`, `sparseResidencyImage3D`, `sampleShading`, `shaderSampleRateShading`, `alphaToCoverage`, `alphaToOne`, `importHandle` and `presentDirect`, and every other flag is false, but the pipeline's; its formats are `rgba8` and `bgra8` (sampled, filtered, drawn into, blended and storage), `bgra8srgb` (the same but for storage), `r8` (sampled, filtered and storage), every other uncompressed colour format the core names (sampled, filtered unless an integer, and a blit's source and destination; the one-channel 8 and 16-bit integers also storage, and the one-channel 32-bit integers storage with atomics), the five depth and stencil formats (sampled, a depth's compared, a blit's source and destination; `depth16`, `depth32f` and `stencil8` also attachments) and the compressed ones the GPU has.
What it refuses, each with words and `.unsupported`: keeping both the depth and the stencil of one image (the frame shader loads one plane); a view that reads a compressed image's blocks as texels, or any texels as another size; a sampler with separate minification and magnification filters (`samplerMinMagFilters`); a one-channel render target; pipeline state beyond the two blends and the triangle list (depth and stencil, culling, polygon modes, depth bias, multisampling, blend factors and masks, logic operations, more than one colour target, other topologies, other stages, specialization constants, dynamic state); the commands the features above name (counted draws, statistics queries).

